🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“Your production server is running at 100% CPU. What will you do?”

I would not immediately restart the server.

First, I want to understand what is actually consuming the CPU.

I would start with:

top

or

htop

Then identify the high-CPU processes:

ps -eo pid,ppid,cmd,%mem,%cpu --sort=-%cpu | head

Once I find the process, I would investigate:

• Is it an application process?
• Did CPU usage suddenly increase?
• Is there a traffic spike?
• Was there a recent deployment?
• Is a process stuck or running continuously?
• Are there errors in the application logs?

I would also check overall server health:

uptime
free -m
df -h
vmstat 1

Then I would check application and system logs around the time the CPU increased.

If an application service is responsible, I would coordinate with the relevant team and follow the approved recovery procedure.

If restarting the affected service is the approved solution, I would restart the service rather than the entire server.

After recovery, I would monitor CPU usage and verify that the application is healthy.

Then comes the important part:

🎯 𝗥𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗖𝗮𝘂𝘀𝗲 𝗔𝗻𝗮𝗹𝘆𝘀𝗶𝘀

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

Because fixing the incident is only part of production support.

The bigger question is:

Why did it happen, and how can we prevent it from happening again?

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

Knowing top is not enough.

Good troubleshooting means knowing what to check, why you're checking it, and what action to take based on the results.
